    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d689">

                <pb facs="0670" n="660" xml:id="pg660"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.154/347</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Adviser on Political Relations</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Hornbeck</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1941-06-12">June 12, 1941</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Mr. Secretary</hi>: I call attention to Major
                    McHugh’s report<anchor n="94" xml:id="d689-f1"/> that the latest tonnage figures
                    for materials carried over the Burma Road for the month of May indicate 19,500
                    tons across the border, with two-thirds of that amount (which would be 13,000
                    tons) reaching Kunming (Yunnanfu, the Chinese terminal).</p>
                <p>Even though there may be some exaggeration, this report is highly significant. A
                    few weeks ago some “wise guy” in the O.P.M.<anchor n="95" xml:id="d689-f2"/>
                    circularized various offices with a memorandum<anchor n="96" xml:id="d689-f3"/>
                    in one paragraph of which he stated that there could not be carried over the
                    Burma Road more than 9,000 tons per month and that what was being carried was
                    considerably less. Shortly thereafter we had a report<anchor n="97"
                        xml:id="d689-f4"/> that there was carried in the month ending April 15 [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">30?</hi>] something over 10,000 tons. Mr. John Earl
                    Baker estimates the potential carrying capacity of the road at 30,000 tons. Some
                    Chinese estimates have put it as high as 50,000 tons.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>S[<hi rendition="#small-caps">tanley</hi>] K. H[<hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">ornbeck</hi>]</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d689-f1" n="94" xml:id="d689-f1-content">
                <p>Report dated June 10, not printed; it was telegraphed to Dr. Currie.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d689-f2" n="95" xml:id="d689-f2-content">
                <p>Office of Production Management.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d689-f3" n="96" xml:id="d689-f3-content">
                <p>Memorandum dated May 8, not printed; but see memorandum of May 29, prepared in
                    the Division of Far Eastern Affairs, <ref target="#pg651">p. 651</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d689-f4" n="97" xml:id="d689-f4-content">
                <p>Despatch No. 238, May 26, from the Consul at Kunming, not printed.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
